name: depth-overlap
description: >-
  Double coverage by depth overlap: a promise is checked by two kits that reach it through
  different layers (the bytes, the program's own state, what it does when driven, what a person
  sees, across a process boundary, or an outside reader) and share no code -- not the harness, and
  not the thing that does the seeing. A fault planted in the layer between the two depths shows
  whether they really overlap (both fire) or only look layered (one is blind). Each kit's language
  is also a seat a domain expert can be invited to take over. Use when mapping what a test suite
  covers, when a check passed over a real fault, on "double coverage", "depth overlap", "is this
  really covered twice", "which kit would catch this", "plant between the layers", "seat a
  language", or before calling a suite layered. Provisional working name; electable.
---

# depth-overlap: two kits, two depths, one promise

**Status:** PROVISIONAL, landed 2026-10-10 on his asking ("I like the idea of double coverage by
depth overlap. I'd like to model that out with a soft skill in mind."). The words below (the six
depths, the observer rule, the seats) are this draft's picks; the questions for his ruling are in
`h/ENHANCE-2026-10-10-depth-overlap.md`, beside the proposed change to grand-test-manifest. First
test case: s1c (`tests/DEPTHS.md` in inputloop/s1c), three planted faults, receipts there.

## The idea in one paragraph

A test reaches a promise through some layer of the work. Two tests in the same layer are one view
twice, however far apart they were written: a fault in a layer neither looks at passes both. Two
tests in different layers, sharing no code, overlap: a fault between them is seen by at least one,
and when they disagree the disagreement says which layer holds the fault. "Double coverage by depth
overlap" is that second arrangement, for the promises that matter most.

## The depths (a general ladder, name your own if the work needs it)

| depth | the check looks at | s1c example |
|---|---|---|
| STRUCTURE | bytes and formats: does it parse, are the sizes and fields right | decompressed art against a reference decompressor |
| STATE | what the program keeps: memory, its log, numbers it computes | work RAM against the real console each frame |
| BEHAVIOUR | what happens when it is driven: positions, flow, menus, timing | holding UP with STEER on moves Sonic 374 px |
| OUTPUT | what a person perceives: pixels, sound | the 3D side view against the classic picture |
| TRANSPORT | the same thing across a process or machine boundary | the netcode over a real, lossy UDP link |
| OUTSIDE | a reader not in the house: the original, a person, another vendor | the emulator running the real ROM |

A kit can reach two depths (the s1c oracle compares RAM and, on request, pixels). Count depths per
promise, not per kit.

## The observer rule

"Share no code" includes **what does the seeing**: the function, library or reference that turns
what happened into a verdict. Two kits in different languages that both call the same checksum are
one observer; they overlap on whatever they drive differently and not at all on what that observer
cannot see. (s1c, 2026-10-10: the in-process netcode test and the real-process one both read the C
checksum of work RAM; a planted fault that changed only the drawn planes passed both, 0 of 2,399
frames flagged, while a probe that also hashed the planes flagged 1,558 and its unplanted control
0.)

## The procedure

1. **List the promises** by name (a TEST MANIFEST's items if there is one; otherwise a plain list).
2. **For each promise, list the kits that reach it**, with the depth each reaches and its observer.
   Say how you know: by reading the kit (OPEN) or by a plant (step 4).
3. **Read the map.** A promise is DOUBLE when two kits reach it at different depths through
   different observers; SAME-DEPTH when its kits share a depth or an observer; SINGLE when one kit
   reaches it; UNCOVERED when none does.
4. **Plant between the layers.** On a copy, never the work, put one fault in the layer between two
   depths (or under the one depth a SINGLE promise has) and run every kit that claims the promise.
   Record FIRED or SURVIVED per kit, as grand-test-manifest does. A pair overlaps only where a plant
   between them fires at least one; a kit that survives a fault in its own promise is BLIND there.
   When a plant survives everything, tell EQUIVALENT (the fault changes nothing) from BLIND (it
   does, and nobody looks) with a probe: a copy that adds the missing look, plus its unplanted
   control. The control must stay quiet, or the probe is measuring noise.
5. **Name the opportunity** for each SINGLE or BLIND promise: the cheapest second depth, the
   observer it would use, and the seat (below) it would suit. Do not build it in the same pass;
   the map is read first.

## Seats: the social half

Every kit is written in a language chosen to differ from the code under test and from the other
kits (the house rule). That costs toolchains; it also opens a seat per language: a place for
someone whose interest is that language's testing practice, at the intersection with this work.
Keep a SEATS list beside the map:

    seat: <language> · kit: <file> · depth(s): <...> · owns: <the question this kit answers>
          open questions an expert would answer better than we do: <...>
          invitation: "great idea -- do you want to take over design of that bit?"

A seat is an invitation, never a claim that someone holds it. Name no person in it until they say
yes. (His words, 2026-10-10: "as many as eleven principle domain experts focused on the intersection
of a given language and testing this".)

## When it is owed

Most promises need one good kit. Double coverage is owed when the promise carries weight across
layers (a view promise that rests on a data promise), when a kit was caught passing over a real
fault (grand-test-manifest's T2), or when the keeper says so. A suite map (steps 1-3) costs an hour
and is worth doing once per work; plants are owed where the map claims DOUBLE.

## How this skill can fail (falsifiers)

- **Layered on paper:** a promise mapped DOUBLE survives a plant between its depths in every kit.
  The map was drawn from the kits' names, not their observers. Check: every DOUBLE row cites a
  plant receipt, or says OPEN.
- **A hidden shared observer:** two kits in a DOUBLE row call the same verdict code. Check: name the
  observer in each row; grep both kits for it.
- **Depths assigned after the fact:** a kit's depth was chosen to make a row look double. Check: the
  depth names what the kit reads (a file, a log line, a pixel), quoted from the kit.
- **A noisy probe:** a probe's unplanted control flags mismatches, so "BLIND" was noise. Check: the
  control's count is in the receipt and is 0.
- **Seats as decoration:** a SEATS list that never reaches a person. This one cannot be judged in a
  day; he asked to see results before assuming what they will say.

Off-switch: say "no depth map on this", or remove this folder.

## LENS (six lines, carried)

```
LENS · depth-overlap · 2026-10-10
a  my view:            two checks only overlap where a fault between them is seen; prove it by planting
b  the view I ascribe: double coverage plus a seat per language; toolchain cost buys friends too
c  their pull on mine: toward a soft skill fed back to the garden, not an s1c note
d  my pull on theirs:  the observer rule and the six depth names are mine until ruled
persona / inverse:     the plant keeps every survivor / the inverse: most promises owe one good kit
principles reframe:    plain words; each seat an open invitation; receipts anyone can rerun
```

